{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact pushed head 8b5dd5ac2f9bd028e2d9aff5a94ad20d645e0be4, resting on all seven named exact-head artifacts.\n\nBASE AND CARRY. The branch is clean and its four implementing commits stand directly on current main de9a2b835ef98ccec2035945eaa016d7e0aafa50. A range-diff against the previously approved carried head 2742fae8 shows the public-surface, failure-mode, and prior-finding repair commits unchanged. The only substantive carry difference is in the extraction commit: collision-safe local writes from ee4ccc8c move from internal/app to internal/apphost, SaveConfig uses that helper, and PublishResident follows it there. The seven artifacts are effective, live, not stale, not describes_superseded_world, and all name this exact head; the old unmerged host and internal/apphost candidates are retired.\n\nPRIOR FINDINGS REMAIN CLOSED. Open first obtains a kernel-verified Log and passes its exact Head to BindingInForce, so a concurrent ref advance cannot choose an interpreter from an unaudited frontier. Append computes the payload-tree identity in memory with HashPayloadTree and leaves the one bounded write to kernel submission; rejected oversized input creates no unreachable objects. The uint64 genesis ceiling is clamped only when crossing into ReadFileLimit's int64 bound, so values above MaxInt64 cannot narrow negative and no realizable Git blob range is lost. Those behaviors and their regressions are byte-identical to the head previously reviewed.\n\nATOMIC-WRITE CARRY. Moving WriteFileAtomically to internal/apphost is the right dependency direction and the simpler result. Repository-private configuration belongs to apphost, while resident advertisement is another file in the same metadata directory; one same-directory helper avoids duplicate implementations across the layer seam. It preserves unique os.CreateTemp names, mode 0600, deferred cleanup, close-before-rename, and atomic same-directory replacement. I independently mutated SaveConfig back to the fixed config.json.tmp name: TestConcurrentLocalSavesUseUniqueTemporaryFiles failed on the fixed name for concurrent writers. Restoring the exact head made it pass and left the review worktree clean. This demonstrates that the carried regression reaches the relocated helper rather than merely passing around it.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are 4, application host binding (internal/apphost); 5, application profile/interpreter (internal/app); and 7, surfaces/composition (host, cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp), with internal/perfscenario adapting mechanically to the layer-4 type move. The public contract changes: an application in another Go module can Init, Open, Append, and read Records while owning its own fold. docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in this same exact head and its candidate is correctly published at the surviving docs path. host and internal/apphost depend on neither internal/app nor internal/workroom; the external-module build and negative internal-import proof make that boundary executable. Kernel ordering and application meaning remain separated. No architecture defect remains.\n\nSECURITY. I re-examined the new module-external trust boundary. Init validates the application identity and initializer key, creates bounded genesis state, and records the binding as the first signed act. Binding authority remains the initializer key; the reserved binding schema cannot be submitted through the application act path. Open verifies the sequencer chain before any interpreter refusal and reads the binding from that exact verified frontier. Append accepts actor keys without a host roster by design, but still signs the complete intent, enforces the genesis payload ceiling and kernel field/reference bounds, uses a bounded sequencer queue, and writes no payload object before validation. Read-only attachments refuse Append explicitly. Records exposes only verified ordered records and documents borrowed ownership; there is no local signed checkpoint, so a fresh process audits from genesis. SourceURL is provenance only and is never fetched; private actor and sequencer keys stay local. The exact-head tests cover wrong application/fold, unverifiable-chain precedence, initializer authority, malformed and unauthorized bindings, reserved schema, read-only custody, ceiling enforcement, refused-write object counts, exact-frontier selection, and external-module isolation. No unresolved security defect remains.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The exported surface is deliberately four acts with the application fold kept outside the module. The apphost extraction removes duplicated binding/configuration vocabulary from the Workroom-coupled adapter, and the atomic-helper relocation removes rather than adds a second implementation. The one-line cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, and internal/perfscenario adaptations are the minimum consequences of the type move. I found nothing cuttable without weakening the external-import proof, trust-boundary regressions, or the documented contract.\n\nINDEPENDENT GATES AT THE EXACT HEAD. go test -count=1 ./... passed all 27 packages, including host external-module proofs and internal/docset. go test -race -count=1 ./host ./internal/app ./internal/apphost passed. go build ./..., go vet ./..., gofmt -l ., git diff --check, and clean-worktree checks passed. Focused mutation and restoration passed as described above.\n\nAll seven reviewed paths are named in this verdict: host d5571785550936bb5dbd5c1f3c91970f0753f4c0; internal/apphost d3c144e93eb0ab3abf7f615cad92291d71409b48; docs 29439846217f934baf0582e15ab6814bf6ca37c8; internal/app 8a256006f3da76924ad982c231b3b29fdc1555a3; cmd/gs 51a73f378dd1532897aa921092ef406d4e4c595d; cmd/gitseq-mcp 5f0340970e3fe76e2701f65415f10f5193a84fd0; internal/perfscenario 22e522fe58eef152ef3ad253c86a11a51db03532. I have not merged.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d5571785550936bb5dbd5c1f3c91970f0753f4c0","head":"8b5dd5ac2f9bd028e2d9aff5a